import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("demo");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
<section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview">
          <p class="docs-kicker">Demo and Sample Report</p>
          <h1>Inspect a real report.<span>No installation needed.</span></h1>
          <p>Inspect before you execute. Repyy identifies risks; it cannot prove that a repository is safe.</p>
          <p>
            <code>cases.json</code> stores eight risky fixture descriptions and eight paired controls as data. The benchmark materializes them in temporary
            directories, invokes only Repyy, and removes them afterward. It never installs packages, starts the project, opens an IDE, runs hooks or starts
            Compose. Do not run assignment-controlled commands or open materialized fixtures in a task-enabled IDE.
          </p>
          <p>Reviewed: 2026-09-15. Benchmark 1.1.0; see the generated results for the scanner build.</p>
          <div class="docs-summary">
            <span><b>8 / 8</b>Expected signals detected</span><span><b>0 / 8</b>Controls flagged high/critical</span><span><b>0</b>Incomplete scans</span>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-callout">
            <strong>Eight inert indicators, with paired controls.</strong>
            <p>This is a small inert benchmark, not a general accuracy score. <a href="./sample/results.json">Inspect every case and finding</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <p><a class="docs-inline-link" href="./sample/sample.html">Open the offline HTML report →</a></p>
          <p>
            <a href="./sample/sample.txt">Terminal output</a> · <a href="./sample/sample.json">JSON report</a> ·
            <a href="./sample/results.md">Benchmark results</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/tree/main/demo">Fixture source</a>
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="safety-design">
          <h2>Safety design</h2>
          <p>
            JavaScript examples use inert strings or harmless local configuration. The startup example imports only its local marker module; reproduction never
            executes it. The lifecycle and hook examples only print markers. The IDE command names a nonexistent marker. The Docker image uses a nonexistent
            image on a reserved <code>.invalid</code> registry. Credential references are fake path strings; no file read or credential collection exists.
            Network/evaluation examples are quoted text, with no callable downloader or execution chain. Fixtures are created without executable permissions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The standalone <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy-demo">repyy-demo repository</a> publishes this inert corpus as benchmark
            <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy-demo/tree/v1.1.0">v1.1.0</a>. Its checked-in reports were generated with the verified official Repyy
            v0.5.1 Linux binary from commit <code>261cc64bde67b9c73ba23021e052c330f64bbd05</code>. The same samples are available on this site.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="real-corpus" data-search="real corpus pinned malicious repositories OpenSSF process-log Axios incomplete coverage">
          <h2>What a real corpus taught us</h2>
          <p>
            The inert benchmark above is reproducible on this site. Separately, a static Phase 1 review scanned the
            <a href="https://github.com/xndbogdan/malicious-repositories/tree/dc82f332dae0f4e9ea6bdc1d8341c7743c59913f">pinned malicious-repositories corpus</a>
            at commit <code>dc82f332</code> with Repyy rules <code>2026.09.19</code>. These are observed gaps from that run, followed by the narrow checks added
            in rules <code>2026.09.26</code>. A local replay was run, but its results have not yet been reviewed and published as the demo. The right column
            describes a capability, not a published measurement.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Pinned corpus evidence" tabindex="0">
            <table class="docs-data-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Static evidence</th><th>Phase 1 observed</th><th>Current rule to review</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>coinpool-rental-platform1.0</code> calls <code>process-log</code> at module load.</td>
                  <td>No package indicator finding.</td>
                  <td>The embedded snapshot now includes the all-version <a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rqwx-v86m-wwff">process-log advisory</a>. Inspect the resolved version and source before any install.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>real-estate-rental-platform</code> passes Axios response data to <code>eval</code>.</td>
                  <td>The execution sink was reported; the fetch-to-execution path was not correlated.</td>
                  <td><a href="/rules/#rule-FLOW-001"><code>FLOW-001</code></a> correlates supported same-function Axios response flows and retains source and sink lines.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>DEX-staking-project-ultrax</code> declares <code>vite-tsconsole-log</code> and contains undecodable locale files.</td>
                  <td>The package indicator was absent; three files were skipped, so the scan was incomplete.</td>
                  <td>The <a href="https://osv.dev/vulnerability/MAL-2025-4289">OSV record</a> is embedded. Undecodable source still makes coverage incomplete.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/ossf/malicious-packages">OpenSSF Malicious Packages</a> is an advisory-report source; it is separate from the
            repository sample corpus. Neither source establishes remote payload contents or a common actor.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="reproduce">
          <h2>Reproduce</h2>
          <p>From the Repyy source checkout, build Repyy itself and run its trusted benchmark runner:</p>
          <pre class="docs-example"><code>go build -o /tmp/repyy ./cmd/repyy
python3 scripts/benchmark.py --binary /tmp/repyy --output /tmp/repyy-demo --check
</code></pre>
          <p>
            The runner invokes only that binary. It writes <code>results.json</code>, <code>results.md</code>, <code>sample.json</code>,
            <code>sample.txt</code> and <code>sample.html</code>. Open the HTML report locally, or inspect the checked-in
            <a href="./sample/sample.html">sample report</a> without installing anything. Generated timestamps identify the run; scan durations and temporary
            root names are normalized for publication. Samples use local fixture identities, not a fabricated remote commit.
          </p>
        </section>
